                <pb facs="0263" n="178" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg178"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">422.11G93/1008</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ecuadoran Minister</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Elizalde</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Translation]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-02-05"><hi rendition="#italic">February 5,
                        1919</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 3</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reply to your kind note No. 44 of
                    January 10 last,<anchor n="10" xml:id="d102-f1"/> I have the honor to confirm my
                    note No. 1 of the 9th of that month<anchor n="11" xml:id="d102-f2"/> and to
                    inform you that I received today from my Government a telegram that a final
                    remittance of 35000 pounds has paid in full the interests [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">coupon of interest</hi>] of the Guayaquil and Quito
                    Railway Company for the year 1918.</p>
                <p>My Government has thus redeemed the promise made to the Department of State to
                    effect the payment on condition that it would receive permission to import 14000
                    tons of cocoa into the United States.</p>
                <p>I avail myself [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">R. H. Elizalde</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
